## Group C: Natural stone (11 October 2026)

Rock surfaces whose relief comes from fracture, bedding, crystal grain and weathering; the decisive measurement is often the spectrum slope (M5), because fracture surfaces are self-affine and their roughness exponent differs by rock type (11 October 2026)

### C1 Marble (11 October 2026)

- **Macro**: a polished slab is almost flat; weathered or honed marble shows the veins in faint relief; veins and swirls come from impurities such as clay, silt, sand, iron oxides and chert that metamorphism mobilized and recrystallized, green tones often from serpentine [S1] (11 October 2026)
- **Meso**: sinuous branching vein bands and healed fracture lines; outdoor marble is damaged by acid rain through carbonation, sulfation and black crust [S1]; differential etching of the veins is UNVERIFIED (11 October 2026)
- **Micro**: an interlocking mosaic of carbonate crystals, medium grained [S1]; a sugary granular surface on weathered stone (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Period**: none (11 October 2026)
- **Joints**: none inside a slab; tile joints follow A6 when the reference is a marble floor (11 October 2026)
- **Profile**: veins as gentle grooves or ridges of very low amplitude with soft edges (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Histogram**: very narrow and unimodal for polished stone, slightly skewed for weathered stone (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Spectrum**: very low energy; the veins add an anisotropic low-frequency component (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Anisotropy**: veins usually share a dominant direction, moderate coherence (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Components**: the vein mask is one or a few elongated connected networks (11 October 2026)
