## Group H: Textiles, hide and animal surfaces (11 October 2026)

Interlaced yarns and the surfaces grown by animals; the families split into lattices of crossings (weave, knit, scales, feathers) identified by M1, and polygonal furrow networks (leather, skin) identified by M7 and M6 (11 October 2026)

### H1 Fabric weave (plain, twill, knit) (11 October 2026)

- **Macro**: the thread count and the weave structure; drape folds do not belong in a tileable height (11 October 2026)
- **Meso, plain weave**: each weft goes over one warp and under the next, the next weft reverses it; a balanced plain weave uses yarns of the same weight with as many ends as picks per inch and looks like a checkerboard; basketweave bundles two or more threads woven as one [S2] (11 October 2026)
- **Meso, twill**: notation n/m gives harnesses raised over lowered per insertion, 2/1 is two up one down on three harnesses, 3/1 is the denim structure, plain weave is 1/1; floats cross two or more yarns and their offset at each row draws the diagonal wale; twill has a distinct face and back and fewer interlacings than plain weave, so it drapes and recovers from creasing better [S1]; the wale angle near 45 degrees for balanced cloth is UNVERIFIED (11 October 2026)
