## Group I: Roofing and thatch (11 October 2026)

Overlapping units laid in courses down a slope; the height is a sawtooth along the slope (each course thickest at its butt), so M3 and M1 along the vertical axis identify the family before any texture (11 October 2026)

### I1 Roof shingles (wood, slate, clay) (11 October 2026)

- **Macro, wood**: shingles are always sawn, shakes are normally split at least on one side, are longer and thicker and more textured because they split along the grain; cedar shingles come in 18 and 24 in lengths (460 and 610 mm) [S1]; exposures and random widths are UNVERIFIED (11 October 2026)
- **Macro, slate**: slate shingles last 80 to 400 years and may be cut in decorative patterns [S1]; double lap exposure equals the slate length minus the headlap, divided by two, so a 20 in slate with a 3 in headlap shows 8.5 in; that rule comes from roofing inspection pages found in search, not from the two cited sources (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)
- **Macro, clay**: plain tiles overlap so that at least two tiles cover every point, some with pointed or rounded beaver-tail bases; Roman roofs lay a semi-cylindrical imbrex over the joints of flat tegulae; mission tiles pair an arched pan and cover; pantiles merge pan and cover into one S shaped piece, subdued in the Scandinavian variant; interlocking tiles (Marseilles, Ludowici) lock to their neighbours [S2] (11 October 2026)
